{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785702206,"nonce":"JhE4WEtibx3YtxkUTb9Y6wKhsCs=","message":"**Policy DECIDED 2026-08-03 (grill session; ADR-0066).** Layered, reachability first, access index DEFERRED:\n\n1. **Evict unreachable first** — point `retention.rs`'s existing mark walk at warm. No new machinery, and it also fixes this ticket's \"retention in warm-only is degenerate\" finding (nothing sweeps warm today at all).\n2. **If still over the high-water mark AND cold exists** — evict reachable-but-cold-backed by recency. THIS is the step that needs the access index, and it is the step we defer, gated on measuring whether (1) suffices. CI generates enormous amounts of unreachable content (every superseded Run's workspaces), so (1) may hold the line for a long time.\n3. **In warm-only, over the mark → REFUSE WRITES LOUDLY.** Warm ENOSPC there means the snapshot is nowhere.\n\nSize watermark, not TTL — the budget is space, the only bound an operator controls and the only one instrumented.\n\n**Correction to this ticket's own text:** `WarmTier::evict`'s \"Safe by construction: cold still holds them\" and `note_warm_write_failure`'s \"the snapshot IS durable\" are NOT false — they are INCOMPLETE. Exactly true with object storage, wrong only in warm-only. **Qualify them, do not delete them.** Same for the UI string at `snapshot-retention.ts:129-131`: make it tier-aware like the durability stamp, rather than removing the claim.\n\n**Pin set is deployment-dependent:** with object storage it is EMPTY (warm is a pure cache, eviction costs a cold fetch and nothing else). In warm-only, eviction is loss — but loss is WITHIN CONTRACT (ADR-0066: warm-only means there is no system of record for workspaces, disclosed per-Attempt), so pins protect only in-flight work.","files":null}]}